        "description": "One complete apparatus: upper-left funnel with a narrow outlet and small grains falling into the upper end of a long downward-sloping open sand chute. One left tripod stand with vertical post, horizontal clamp arm, collar and visible clamp/screw projections supports that chute. Grains leaving the lower chute mouth hit the left face of one vertically oriented oblique disc, then fall beneath it. The disc is attached to one thin straight rod extending upward-right through a bearing/support region and continuing past the spring plate attachment. One right tripod stand carries a curved open bracket/bearing, a vertical dial support, and a thick diagonal lower brace reaching the lower spring-plate clamp. An unnumbered curved dial has six visible tall graduation strokes and twenty shorter strokes; the slanting pointer partly overlaps the right graduation region. No scale values, units or specified reading. One pointer/lever extends from the rod/bearing region to that dial. One narrow curved spring plate runs from the rod attachment region down to a lower clamp; the original nearby glyph is П/Π-shaped. No sand-force or flow arrow is printed. Source black fills/hatching are material depiction, not additional apparatus, calibrated marks or quantities. Modern additions: Deterministic clean unmeasured apparatus contours. One English Spring plate label with a plain identification leader replaces the uncertain original glyph. Particle/material stroke counts are schematic, not gas data. Uncertainties: The original spring-plate glyph is visually compatible with Cyrillic П and Greek Π. Neither alphabet identity is asserted, and no original alphabet label is emitted. Pointer overlap does not specify an exact dial reading or any hidden subdivision. Visible unnumbered graduations remain qualitative.",
